WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.400
How can a cell stay ready to destroy itself within hours, yet never trigger that

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:08.400
switch by mistake?

3
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.740
Animal cells carry killer enzymes called caspases.

4
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.580
They cut other proteins apart.

5
00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.200
Unlike a phosphate tag, no enzyme can rejoin a cut peptide bond.

6
00:00:22.283 --> 00:00:26.543
A kinase adds a phosphate, and a phosphatase takes it right back off.

7
00:00:27.803 --> 00:00:29.263
A caspase cut is permanent.

8
00:00:30.643 --> 00:00:34.083
Once caspases start cutting, the cell cannot undo the damage.

9
00:00:35.363 --> 00:00:40.463
To act fast yet stay safe, every caspase is made ahead of time as an inactive

10
00:00:40.463 --> 00:00:45.723
precursor. Those stored precursors wait silently throughout the cytosol.

11
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:49.507
Apoptotic caspases come in two designs.

12
00:00:50.907 --> 00:00:55.547
Initiator caspases, such as caspase-8, float as single inactive chains.

13
00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:01.827
Executioner caspases, such as caspase-3, are already paired into dimers.

14
00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:08.307
Surprisingly, those pairs stay silent because an uncut linker locks each active site

15
00:01:08.307 --> 00:01:12.127
shut. Only a cut unlocks them to slice targets.

16
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.180
That puts the key decision on the initiator chains.

17
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.840
To switch on, two initiator chains must meet and pair up.

18
00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.440
In a healthy cell, those single chains drift far apart across the cytosol.

19
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.580
Pause and think: why don't they pair up and fire on their own?

20
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.640
Because they are too dilute to hold together on their own.

21
00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.300
Random bumps separate before anything happens.

22
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.160
To switch them on, the cell builds an assembly platform that packs copies side by

23
00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:53.540
side. Crowded together, the chains pair up and activate.

24
00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.220
So for initiators, the first step is a gathering, not a cut.

25
00:01:59.283 --> 00:02:02.383
One way to build that platform starts outside the cell.

26
00:02:03.563 --> 00:02:07.443
A killer T cell presents a three-part signal called Fas ligand.

27
00:02:08.563 --> 00:02:14.403
It binds a surface death receptor named Fas and pulls several Fas receptors into a

28
00:02:14.403 --> 00:02:20.383
cluster. A two-ended bridge protein called FADD docks onto those clustered receptor

29
00:02:20.383 --> 00:02:24.583
tails. Its other end grabs inactive caspase-8.

30
00:02:25.617 --> 00:02:30.977
Receptor, bridge, and initiator together form one platform: the death-inducing

31
00:02:30.977 --> 00:02:33.017
signaling complex, or DISC.

32
00:02:34.257 --> 00:02:39.137
Inside the DISC, caspase-8 chains sit side by side, pair up, and switch on.

33
00:02:40.417 --> 00:02:43.217
No kinase or second messenger is needed here.

34
00:02:44.477 --> 00:02:49.997
Active caspase-8 then clips the linker on executioner dimers, unlocking them to cut.

35
00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:56.270
Unlocked executioners cut hundreds of structural proteins and free an enzyme that

36
00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:03.670
chops DNA. Unlike an injured necrotic cell that swells and bursts, an apoptotic cell

37
00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:06.210
shrinks into neat, membrane-wrapped parcels.

38
00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:11.490
Neighbours swallow those parcels, so usually no inflammation follows.

39
00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:17.310
Because cuts are permanent, gathering initiators on a platform holds the trigger.
